      "prompt": "(a) Write the word equation or symbol equation for aerobic respiration and say where in the cell it happens. (b) Say where in the cell aerobic respiration happens and where anaerobic respiration happens. (c) Compare aerobic and anaerobic respiration in yeast: oxygen needed, products, and ATP yield per glucose. (d) Describe one Kenyan example where anaerobic respiration is useful.",
      "exemplar": "(a) Glucose + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water + energy. In symbols: C6H12O6 + 6O2 → 6CO2 + 6H2O + energy. It happens in the mitochondria. (b) Aerobic respiration happens in the mitochondria and needs oxygen. Anaerobic respiration in yeast and plant cells happens in the cytoplasm and needs no oxygen. (c) Aerobic respiration needs oxygen. It produces carbon dioxide and water and gives about 36 to 38 ATP per glucose. Anaerobic respiration in yeast happens without oxygen in the cytoplasm. It produces ethanol and carbon dioxide (C6H12O6 → 2C2H5OH + 2CO2 + energy) and gives only 2 ATP per glucose. (d) Mandazi dough rises because yeast ferments sugar without oxygen and the carbon dioxide forms gas pockets. Other examples are souring of uji, brewing busaa, biogas digesters in Kisumu, and ethanol production. In waterlogged soil, plant roots may also respire anaerobically."
